 ## Overview
-Unbiased Line Integration is a method for estimating the transmittance-weighted integral along a light ray in participating media, specifically designed to split primary rays and estimate contributions from both the volume and the surface boundary [S1]. It solves the problem of accurately computing the integral $C$ defined in the radiative transfer context without the bias introduced by fixed-step ray marching [S1]. Unlike [Analytic Transmittance Integration](5116-analytic-transmittance-integration.md) which relies on closed-form solutions, or [Heterogeneous Medium Transmittance](4909-heterogeneous-medium-transmittance.md) which may use different sampling strategies, this method uses transformed equidistant sampling to achieve smoothness approaching that of ray marching while remaining perfectly unbiased [S1].
